<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext and a development certificate authority, for development only.

  This file replaces the one in `main` for debug builds. It permits plain HTTP to
  anything, which is what a server on the same desk needs: `10.0.2.2` for the emulator,
  and whatever `192.168.x.x` a handset on the same Wi-Fi has to use.

  It cannot reach a release build — a different source set is a different resource — and
  `ServerAddress.allowsCleartext()` is keyed on `BuildConfig.DEBUG` for the same reason.
  Both halves have to agree, and both are compiled out of a shipped app.

  ## Why the trust anchors are here

  A development server with TLS is served a certificate from a local authority — mkcert,
  or whatever the person running it uses. **Android does not trust user-installed
  authorities for app traffic**, and has not since Android 7: a CA added through Settings
  is trusted by the browser and by nothing else. So `https://10.0.2.2:8443` fails to
  verify even after the certificate is installed, which reads as "the certificate is
  wrong" when what is wrong is that nobody asked the app to trust it.

  Naming `user` here is what asks. `system` stays listed because leaving it out would
  mean a debug build could not reach a real server with a real certificate — which is
  the configuration most worth testing before shipping.

  This is the whole reason `debug-overrides` exists, and why it is refused in a release
  build: the platform will not let this ship even by accident.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />

    <debug-overrides>
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="user" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </debug-overrides>
</network-security-config>
